========================================================================
WHY THE FAMILY SPLIT MATTERS (2.9 attack class)
========================================================================

Three rails, one name-set that **does not match**:

| Rail | Names that get **secret** IDENT refuse |
|------|----------------------------------------|
| check / llvm front-door (`check_secret_println_sinks` L175–192) | **`println` `eprintln` only** (alias-resolved sid) |
| stmt emit (`c_emit_ident_stmt` → `c_emit_println`) | **`println` only** (`__sec__` IDENT; **no** alias resolve) |
| free-call / method (`c_emit_lower` / `c_emit_call` → guard) | `println` `eprintln` **`print_err` `log` `fprintf` `syslog`** (+ other sealed) |

`eprintln` is a **product** sibling (`tc_names`, LLVM builtin,
`SECRET_TAINT` In). `print_err` / `log` / `fprintf` / `syslog` are
**emit-guard only**. `print` is LLVM-only shape. 2.9 is the unify of
those rails — **not** a new refuse for println (already dual-path).

